---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageFrame from '../../../components/PageFrame.astro';
import snapshot from '../../../data/project-progress.json';
import { validateProgressSnapshot } from '../../../lib/project-progress.mjs';
import '../../../styles/project-progress.css';
const progress = validateProgressSnapshot(snapshot);
const workflows = progress.capabilities.filter(c => c.id !== 'aabb');
const supporting = progress.projects.filter(p => !['code', 'emu', 'asm'].includes(p.id));
---
<Layout title="Project progress — the 198x family" description="Where Code198x, Emu198x and Asm198x stand, what works together and what needs attention next.">
  <PageFrame as="article" class="project-progress">
    <header class="progress-opening">
      <a href="/family">The 198x family</a>
      <h1>Where we stand.</h1>
      <p class="lead">We have curriculum to learn from, machines to run it on, and tools to build it. The next step is to make those parts dependable together.</p>
      <p>Code198x needs learner and content acceptance. Emu198x needs complete usability evidence for named configurations. Asm198x needs compatibility evidence connected to real authoring work.</p>
      <p>The checkpoints below can change as we learn more. They give us something concrete to assess without assigning an overall completion score.</p>
      <p class="progress-note">Snapshot checked <time datetime={progress.checked_on}>{progress.checked_on}</time>. These are editorial assessments grounded in the linked records, with evidence available at that date. This page does not run fresh tests when you open it.</p>
      <nav aria-label="Project progress"><a href="#code">Code198x</a><a href="#emu">Emu198x</a><a href="#asm">Asm198x</a><a href="#workflows">Working together</a><a href="/family/progress/evidence">Detailed evidence</a></nav>
    </header>
    {progress.briefing.map(p => (
      <section class="progress-project" id={p.id} aria-labelledby={`${p.id}-heading`}>
        <h2 id={`${p.id}-heading`}>{p.name}</h2>
        <p class="progress-purpose">{p.purpose}</p>
        <h3>{p.headline}</h3>
        {p.assessment_state !== 'current' && <p class="progress-alert">Assessment needs review. Its source records changed or were unavailable at the snapshot check; read this as a dated assessment.</p>}
        <div class="progress-columns">
          <div>
            <p><strong>Where we are.</strong> {p.assessment}</p>
            <p><strong>What we can show.</strong> {p.demonstrated}</p>
            <p><strong>What needs attention.</strong> {p.gap}</p>
          </div>
          <aside aria-label={`Working next checkpoint for ${p.name}`}>
            <h4>Working next checkpoint</h4>
            <p>{p.checkpoint}</p><p>{p.checkpoint_reason}</p>
            <p class="progress-note">A recommendation for review; we can revise this as priorities change.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>
        <p class="progress-note">Assessment reviewed <time datetime={p.reviewed_on}>{p.reviewed_on}</time> · {p.assessment_state === 'current' ? 'Source records unchanged at the snapshot check.' : 'Assessment needs review.'} This checks the records, not the software again.</p>
        <details><summary>Sources behind this assessment</summary><ul>{p.sources.map(s => <li><a href={s.url}>{s.label}</a> — {s.matches ? 'unchanged at snapshot' : 'changed or unavailable'}</li>)}</ul></details>
      </section>
    ))}
    <section class="progress-section" id="workflows" aria-labelledby="workflows-heading">
      <h2 id="workflows-heading">What works together</h2>
      <p>Four selected programming patterns connect the curriculum, assembler and emulator; the Amiga example also uses Build198x. These are demonstrated paths through the family, with named versions and configurations.</p>
      <table><caption>Retained execution results, source-checked at the snapshot date</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Connected projects</th><th scope="col">Evidence state</th></tr></thead><tbody>{workflows.map(c => <tr><th scope="row"><a href={`/family/progress/evidence#${c.id}`}>{c.title}</a></th><td>Code198x → Asm198x → {c.id === 'amiga' && 'Build198x → '}Emu198x</td><td>{c.state_label}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
      <p>This establishes a useful connection, not universal compatibility. AABB also has a host-Python check. <a href="/family/progress/evidence">Inspect all five results, versions and limits.</a></p>
    </section>
    <section class="progress-section" aria-labelledby="attention-heading">
      <h2 id="attention-heading">Where to put attention</h2>
      <p>My recommendation is to deepen one complete learner-to-running-program journey before adding more breadth to this view. It gives all three projects a shared, observable checkpoint.</p>
      <p>Keep the tests underneath that journey: source compatibility, program behaviour and usable interaction each need their own evidence. Learner trials, listening and original-hardware checks remain distinct from automated checks.</p>
      <p>When the goalposts move, revise the working checkpoint and review its sources. Existing evidence keeps its original meaning; a changed goal does not rewrite a historical result.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="progress-section" aria-labelledby="supporting-heading">
      <h2 id="supporting-heading">The rest of the family</h2>
      <p>These projects supply tools, shared contracts and media capabilities around the three priorities. The activity labels describe declared project activity, not readiness.</p>
      <details><summary>Seven other projects and their roles</summary><table><caption>Family roles and declared activity at snapshot</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Project</th><th scope="col">Role</th><th scope="col">Activity</th></tr></thead><tbody>{supporting.map(p => <tr><th scope="row"><a href={p.url}>{p.name}</a></th><td>{p.role}</td><td>{p.phase === 'active' ? 'Active' : 'Deferred'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></details>
    </section>
    <p class="progress-note"><a href="/family/progress/snapshot.json">Download the reviewed snapshot as JSON.</a> Sources and measurements retain their original dates and scope.</p>
  </PageFrame>
</Layout>
